WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:08.820
Around 251 million years ago, the sun rose
on the Triassic period.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.650
Although often overlooked, the Triassic marked
the evolution of some of the most unique organisms

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.490
in our planet’s history — especially under
the waves.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:22.999
Within the waters of the Triassic dwelt reptiles
with hammer-shaped faces, fish with buzz-saw

00:00:22.999 --> 00:00:28.550
like teeth which confounded scientists for
centuries, and some of the largest — and

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.999
most often forgotten — marine predators
of all time.

00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:37.170
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
be winding back the clock and looking at the

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.480
undersea organisms of the Triassic.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.870
The Triassic Period began with a global cataclysm.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:49.370
An extinction event known as the Great Dying,
caused by elevated temperatures and oceanic

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:55.500
acidification, resulted in over ninety percent
of all life in the oceans, including the long-enduring

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.559
trilobites, dying out.

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:02.980
But in the wake of this great reset, new species
emerged, growing to fill the abundance of

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:08.180
unoccupied niches — resulting in some truly
surreal adaptations.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:14.090
This is Henodus, a placodont from the late
Triassic that likely dwelled in the shallows.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.020
Henodus, like most members of its order, resembles
a sea turtle — but isn’t actually closely

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.020
related.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.719
Henodus was around 1 meter, or 3.3 feet in
length — and that distinctive, unwieldy

00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:30.110
shell on their back was almost as wide as
it was long.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:35.439
Exactly why Henodus… or any animal… would
develop such an unusual shell isn’t fully

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:36.439
understood.

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:41.680
But some have theorized the Henodus’s shell
was such an awkward size, it made it difficult

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.399
for aquatic predators to fit their jaws around
it.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:48.320
A very straightforward strategy, but a pretty
smart one!

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.310
Aside from Henodus, the Placodont order during
the Jurassic Period also included Cyamodus.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:59.170
This genus also looks quite similar to sea
turtles, and might actually be more closely

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.109
related to modern turtles than Henodus.

00:02:02.109 --> 00:02:06.759
Unlike most species of turtles today, however,
which have just a single shell for protection

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.870
— Cyamodus had two separate ones.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:14.140
One shell protected its neck to its waist,
while the other one protected its lower-hip

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.140
region.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.730
The dual shells were likely an effective defense
against predators, and gives Cyamodus a one-of-a-kind

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:21.730
look.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.200
Moving away from the Placodont order, also
living along the Triassic coastline was the

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.430
noteworthy Mastodonsaurus.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:33.340
A triangular headed amphibian, Mastodonsaurus
had two distinctive long front teeth which

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.290
likely helped it capture prey.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.600
Strangely, however, Mastodonsaurus’s teeth
were so long that — in addition to the animal’s

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.380
nostrils – there were two extra holes in
their skull which these teeth fit through

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.190
when their mouths were closed.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:53.570
The Mastodonsaurus’s peculiar upwards facing
eyes also sets them apart from many species.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:58.620
The unexpected eye placement would have been
most suited for looking upwards, suggesting

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.260
the animal often fed on organisms which dwelled
on land.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:08.390
And with the largest Mastodonsaurus’s reaching
around 6 meters, or twenty feet, there’s

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:12.010
quite a lot that would likely be on the menu
for this species.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:18.060
Further out into the ocean, we have an animal
which, for centuries, baffled scientists.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.440
Meet the helicoprion, an animal with one of
the most unusual dental records in the history

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.870
of life on earth.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:30.180
The fish possessed a strange spiral of teeth,
with most geneses possessing more than a hundred

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.260
teeth in total.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:36.120
When fossils of this tooth-spiral were first
uncovered in the 19th century, paleontologists

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:42.400
weren’t sure quite where on the animal it
belonged, with some hypothesizing the tooth-whorl

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.450
sprouted from the animal’s upper snout…
like a really deadly nose.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.480
By the mid 20th century, it became more widely
understood that the fanged-swirl belonged

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.090
on the bottom jaw of the animal.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:57.450
The exact purpose of the spiral was still
unknown, however, with some speculating that

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.690
the spiral of teeth could lash out like a
tentacle to grab prey.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:06.590
Today, most scientific reconstructions place
the spiral of teeth further back in the animal’s

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:09.260
jaws, or perhaps even in its throat.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.960
The most prominent modern theory is that the
tooth-whorl has something to do with replacing

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.290
old teeth — kind of like the system sharks
have.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:21.340
And yes: despite appearances, the helicoprion
is not a genus of shark, but instead thought

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.819
to fall within the now primarily extinct classification
of ratfish.

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:28.139
A somewhat unfortunate name.

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:33.340
Even more unfortunate, helicoprion was already
on its way out when the Triassic kicked off.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:38.759
We think a few species within the Helicoprion
genus made it all the way to early the Triassic

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:43.849
period, but sadly, none of these buzz-saw
faced fish endured any further.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:48.740
And while we’re talking about species with
unusual lips, the Atopodentatus is near the

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.900
top of that particular category.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.710
Older reconstructions of this extinct genus
of middle Triassic marine animal are quite

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.039
the sight to behold.

00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:03.830
At first, scientists believed that, unlike
almost all other animals, Atopodentatus’s

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:08.729
jaw was split down the middle — with teeth
running along the length of this split.

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:14.520
As alien and alarming as this might seem,
most scientists thought Atopodentatus used

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:21.939
its unusual mouth for filter-feeding — using
the toothed split to filter out unwanted dirt.

00:05:21.939 --> 00:05:28.000
New, more complete specimens, however, have
demonstrated Atopodentatus’s split mouth

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.690
was actually a misinterpretation — and the
animal head actually looked like… this.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:39.501
The unusual, wide mouth was possibly an adaptation
to better scrape algae off the sea-floor for

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:42.520
consumption, although we can’t say for certain.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.180
While the new Atopodentatus model is less
frightening than the old, it’s definitely

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.349
just as unique-looking.

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:55.099
Before we get to the true giants of the sea,
our next group might be a bit more familiar

00:05:55.099 --> 00:05:56.099
looking.

00:05:56.099 --> 00:06:02.009
These are the Sauropterygia, a classification
that first appeared at the very start of the

00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:03.009
Triassic.

00:06:03.009 --> 00:06:07.469
Orders like the Nothosaur navigated the waters
on paddle like flippers — snatching fish

00:06:07.469 --> 00:06:10.650
in their fanged jaws with lunges of their
long neck.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:15.979
The Nothosaur likely lived a bit like the
seals of today, as they were rela small — only

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:18.889
reaching 3 meters, or ten feet in length.

00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:25.240
By contrast, the Sauropterygia classification
eventually yielded the much larger Plesiosauria,

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:30.449
a group which is much more commonly known,
and the only branch of Sauropterygia to make

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:32.229
it out of the Triassic.

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:37.659
Most of the biggest and most famous Plesiosauria,
like the Liopleurodon, lived during the Jurassic

00:06:37.659 --> 00:06:43.490
period, and maxed out at 6.4 meter, or 21
feet in length.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.150
Quite the impressive reptile.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:52.939
But even Jurassic-era Plesiosauria were dwarfed
by the true titans of the Triassic waters.

00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:59.770
Meet the Ichthyosaurs — a diverse group
of reptiles with a long paleontological history.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:05.159
Ichthyosaur fossils were discovered as far
back as the 19th century, which led to some

00:07:05.159 --> 00:07:10.330
intriguing vintage artistic interpretations
of what these colossal reptiles might have

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.880
looked like.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.059
Mary Anning found various nearly-complete
skeletons during her lifetime.

00:07:16.059 --> 00:07:20.379
If you’re not familiar with Mary Anning,
she was a pioneering English paleontologist

00:07:20.379 --> 00:07:25.279
who made vital contributions to the field
9though never got the credit she deserved).

00:07:25.279 --> 00:07:31.520
One skeleton Anning found was of a Temnodontosaurus,
an Ichthyosaur genus from the early Jurassic.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:38.159
In their overall body-shape and long, toothed
beaks, these unique animals resemble dolphins.

00:07:38.159 --> 00:07:43.159
While the two animals occupy a similar niche,
they aren’t related, and are just an interesting

00:07:43.159 --> 00:07:47.850
example of two different animals converging
on a similar body plan due to environmental

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.080
factors.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:54.250
Unlike the dolphin, however, the Temnodontosaurus
got much bigger, ranging somewhere between

00:07:54.250 --> 00:08:01.300
9 to 12 meter in size, or 30 to 40 ft — easily
the largest organism on our list so far.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:05.650
But the very largest of the Ichthyosaurus
were in another class altogether.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:11.729
Meet the Shonisaurus, a Late Triassic genus,
coming in somewhere between 15 and 20 meters,

00:08:11.729 --> 00:08:15.289
or 49 to 69 ft.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:18.229
What an incredible creature.

00:08:18.229 --> 00:08:23.460
Like the smaller Temnodontosaurus, the Shonisaurus
was likely also a marine predator who hunted

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.309
for fish, cephalopods, and other forms of
smaller marine life.

00:08:27.309 --> 00:08:33.339
After all, there’s not much that could stand
up to an organism of this size.

00:08:33.339 --> 00:08:38.950
Indeed, the Shonisaurus rivals many of the
largest modern whales with their sheer bulk.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:44.631
Another way in which they’re similar to
whales is that the ancestors of Shonisaurus,

00:08:44.631 --> 00:08:49.500
and indeed all Ichthyosaurs, once lived on
land before transitioning to fully aquatic

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.500
animals.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.380
Cartorhynchus is a potential distant relative
of the Ichthyosaur beginning the land-to-ocean

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.780
transition process.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.460
While Cartorhynchus likely spent most of their
time in the water, it’s been theorized they

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:06.580
could still use their flipper-like forelimbs
to move on land, sort of like juvenile sea

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:07.580
turtles.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:11.820
Unlike many of the other groups on this list,
various species of Ichthyosaurs survived beyond

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:12.850
the Triassic period.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:18.280
And despite being group’s evolutionary debut,
the Triassic period was the epoch in which

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:20.520
the largest Ichthyosaurs reigned.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.150
This goes to show you that the Triassic, while
often overlooked, was an incredible era in

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:27.340
earth’s history.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:32.310
And that’s the end of our deep dive into
the oceans of the Triassic period.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:36.760
This video was made as part of the Triassic-themed
week E.D.G.E. is currently running, so go

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.090
check out his channel if you haven’t already.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.330
In the meantime, thanks for watching.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:46.490
If you liked this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:49.380
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.290
See you in the next video.

